From 2b3c4d5e6f708192a3b4c5d6e7f8091a2b3c4d5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Haiku Graviton <graviton@localhost>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:30:00 +0000
Subject: cmake/config.guess: recognise Haiku on arm64

Same defect as llvm12-12.0.1-config-guess-arm64.patch, still present three
years later: the config.guess LLVM vendors under cmake/ is dated 2024-06-07 and
still enumerates only the two Haiku hosts that existed when the entry was
written, BePC and x86_64. On arm64 "uname -m" reports "arm64", so no case
matches, the script prints its "unable to guess system type" banner and exits
non-zero. cmake/modules/GetHostTriple.cmake (via config-ix.cmake) treats a
non-zero exit as fatal, so configure dies with "Failed to execute
cmake/config.guess" before a single object is compiled.

Note this is LLVM's *own* trimmed copy, not the system one. The three
config.guess copies Haiku ships (autoconf, automake, libtool build-aux) all
carry an upstream "*:Haiku:*:*" catch-all and answer correctly, so ports that
regenerate with autoreconf are unaffected -- only a bundled stale copy like this
one breaks.

Emit aarch64-unknown-haiku rather than the arm64-unknown-haiku that the generic
catch-all would produce: aarch64-unknown-haiku is what the toolchain itself
reports from "gcc -dumpmachine" (verified on hrev59996), and a host triple that
disagrees with the compiler's own makes LLVM's later target checks inconsistent.
A generic Haiku fallback follows it so a future architecture degrades to a
plausible guess instead of aborting the configure outright.

diff --git a/llvm/cmake/config.guess b/llvm/cmake/config.guess
--- a/llvm/cmake/config.guess
+++ b/llvm/cmake/config.guess
@@ -1256,6 +1256,13 @@
     x86_64:Haiku:*:*) # Haiku running on x86_64.
 	echo x86_64-unknown-haiku
 	exit ;;
+    arm64:Haiku:*:*)	# Haiku on arm64. uname -m reports "arm64", but
+			# the toolchain triple is aarch64-unknown-haiku.
+	echo aarch64-unknown-haiku
+	exit ;;
+    *:Haiku:*:*)	# Any other Haiku architecture.
+	echo ${UNAME_MACHINE}-unknown-haiku
+	exit ;;
     SX-4:SUPER-UX:*:*)
 	echo sx4-nec-superux${UNAME_RELEASE}
 	exit ;;
--
